Hmmm, interesting. I usually orgainse things by theme rather than by
type- after all the game's inventory and container management systems
organise stuff by type for you anyway. I usually do it something like
this (depending on how many chests I have access to, and this system is
under constant revision as I play anyway). Bear in mind I play slashem,
and only as a wizard. I also like to keep a *very* tidy dungeon- I will
drag almost every item I can lay my hands on to my stash.

1 - Junk. Contains worthless glass, wands of nothing, spent magic
markers, useless amulets (for a post-quest wizard, any amulet is pretty
much useless unless you want to eat one for magic breathing) and the
vast majority of all the weapons/ armour in the dungeon.
2 - Poly. Contains elvish weps/ armour (particularly positively
enchanted stuff) as well as mirrors, credit cards, wands of invisible,
rings of hunger and anything else that's only good for polymorphing.
I'll also have wands and potions of polymorph in here, along with
potions of restore ability. On these polymorph sessions I'm generally
hoping to come up with silver daggers, +7 speed boots/ GoDex/ HoBrill/
Robe of Power, a wand of wishing or yet another magic marker.
3 - Slaughter. I'll make an icebox by dipping a chest in GL and use it
to store corpses (a few fresh pudding corpses are a must, in case I
ever need to appease Anhur) along with wands of undead turning and
sources of create monster (wands, cursed scrolls of CM, create horde,
cursed ?oGenocide, cursed bell, BoT) for my slaughter sessions. With
pudding farming made useless in slashem, the only way to farm for items
is with create monster. I generally lock myself in a cupboard, generate
a few hundred monsters and then wade out into them. I stopped doing
this on my stash levels when I found a gelatinous cube devouring all my
carefully organised chests. When I run out of charges for CM and my pow
is low, I might camp out in a vault or do the lycanthrope tango for a
little while, but eventually I'll just go out dungeoning until I find
some more means of generating monsters en mass. Better yet, I'll
descend to an appropriate level (sunless sea is good for this- with WW
boots you can retreat over the water when HP are low), reverse-genocide
Master Lichs and let them do the summoning!
4 - Library. All my spellbooks.
5 - Weapons. Artifact weapons and my original +1 quarterstaff (for
nostalgia), along with every single (orcish|elven) dagger found in the
game so far. I like to pile these into huge stacks, #dip them all up to
elven daggers, enchant as many as possible up to >+7 and then dip them
further up to great daggers. ?oEW will be stored here, along with !oOil
(for de-rusting) and maybe a few wands of cancellation.
6 - Armour. Contains any worthwhile armour I'm not currently wearing/
carrying. This includes items that can be upgraded into worthwhile
armour (ie dragon scales, leather gloves) in case I get an abundance of
scrolls and decide to vape some stuff in an attempt to get +7 GoDex or
something. Scrolls of enchant armour and cursed scrolls of destroy
armour are stored here. If I'm feeling particularly geeky I'll remove/
replace all my dragon scales in order to sort them by colour in the
container's contents list.
7 - Dip. Contains otherwise useless potions and scrolls, ready for my
next pond-run. Depending on what spells and intrinsics I have (and what
smoky happens to  be), this might include magic mapping, obj detect,
see invisible...
8 - Loot. Everything else, basically, which boils down to cash, food,
all remaining scrolls, potions and wands, useful or edible rings
(increase damage- yum), wands, magic markers, tinning kits, spare magic
whistles and stehoscopes, tools and usually some other miscellaneous
objects that might be good for upgrade (touchstone, tin opener, glass
orb, mundane bag).

I'll also keep a statue 'room' nearby (although I tend to dig out my
stash levels entirely, so the word 'room' is used generously- it's more
likely to be some sort of boulder enclosure) and a handy pre-assembled
boulder fort or two. 

